<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dalai Lama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dalai Lama is the title of the leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, later the head of the Tibetan government. The institution itself arose in the sixteenth century. The site traces its relationship with Chinese imperial rule to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>'s patronage of Tibetan Buddhist lamas, which set the pattern for later Sino-Tibetan relations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dalai-lama.jpg" alt="Dalai Lama" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christopher Michel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Dalai_Lama_in_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Dalai Lama is part of the full title Holiness Knowing Everything Vajradhara Dalai Lama, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> gave to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a>, the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, in 1578 at Yanghua Monastery. Dalai combines the Mongolic word for ocean with the Tibetan bla-ma, master. Sonam Gyatso had just given teachings to the Khan, and the title was extended to the whole tulku lineage, so he became the 3rd Dalai Lama, while the first two were awarded it after their deaths. The 1st, Gendun Drup (1391 to 1474), was a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a> and abbot of Drepung, and the 2nd established the method of finding later incarnations through visions at the oracle lake, Lhamo Lhatso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The 4th Dalai Lama, Yonten Gyatso (1589 to 1617), was a Mongol, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>, and his connection led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> to support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> zealously. The Gelug were the newest school and lacked an established network of Tibetan clan patronage, so they relied more on foreign patrons. After his death the Tsangpa rulers of Central Tibet forbade a search for his incarnation, but in 1618 the child was secretly identified and later recognised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>, Lobsang Gyatso.</p><p>From 1642 to 1951 the Dalai Lama led the secular government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, directly or through regents, in the government known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a>, which governed the Tibetan Plateau with varying degrees of autonomy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> established control over Tibet in 1720, and Chinese authorities have asserted the right to oversee the selection of spiritual leaders since the 18th century, a practice formalised in 1793. The 6th Dalai Lama, Tsangyang Gyatso, gave up his monk's robes in 1702 and chose a layman's life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Since the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> in the 17th century the Dalai Lama has been a symbol of the unification of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. His religious authority goes beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> tradition and represents Buddhist values not tied to a single school. The extent and nature of the Dalai Lama's secular and religious power remains contested. One interpretation is the mchod yon, usually translated as priest and patron relationship, an alliance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> leaders and secular rulers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> and Chinese authorities. Critics, including Sam van Schaik, hold that the theory oversimplifies the situation, and historians such as Melvyn Goldstein have called Tibet a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state or tributary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dalai Lama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oirat lama Zaya Pandita created the Clear Script (todo bichig) in 1648 to write the Oirat language and translated many Buddhist texts into it, monasteries were built, and close ties to the <b>Dalai Lamas</b> gave the khans religious legitimacy across the Mongol world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Mongol khans, who created the title of <b>Dalai Lama</b>, and the Qing emperors revived it, and it governed Sino-Tibetan relations for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In exile Bonpos were stigmatised and for twenty years received none of the aid that passed through the <b>Dalai Lama</b>’s office, until in 1979 the 14th Dalai Lama advised the Central Tibetan Administration to accept them.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In China and Japan, monasteries held land, ran charitable works and sometimes wielded political and military power; in Tibet, the great monasteries and their lamas became the core of the state, and from the 17th century the <b>Dalai Lamas</b> held both religious and political authority.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In Tibet the <b>Dalai Lama</b> headed a government, the Ganden Phodrang, in which a council of ministers, the Kashag, and parallel ranks of monk and lay officials administered the country.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1578 Altan Khan gave the Tibetan lama Sonam Gyatso the title of <b>Dalai Lama</b>, and Mongolia turned to Tibetan Buddhism.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
